sh\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"86b3adf87de7af9e693f5a16b5336538\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:49',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1548,1,14,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_32e5c26c147d5835d1fe562022165fe0\",\"label\":\"Conditions Governing Access\",\"type\":\"accessrestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The papers are open for research.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"28a4808dc499042271195b5c92be6b4e\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:49',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1549,1,14,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_cf447cf15ada05b551ce18042bb05521\",\"label\":\"Method of Acquisition\",\"type\":\"acqinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The papers were donated by Ernest Rodriguez (donor no. 995) in 2005 and subsequent years.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"9a42f7941b214078529cd174312fc074\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:49',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1550,1,14,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_13bac0b874e78f4d97b86e8bdbfe53fb\",\"label\":\"Preferred Citation\",\"type\":\"prefercite\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Ernest Rodriguez papers, Iowa Women\'s Archives, University of Iowa Libraries, Iowa City\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"b04506aaf4690112868890a19048d8e8\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:49',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1551,1,14,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_f0aadd17aa1318b8aecc553da43ea000\",\"label\":\"Related Materials\",\"type\":\"relatedmaterial\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Estefania Joyce Rodriguez papers: Ernest Rodriguez\'s sister.\\n\\nLULAC Council #10 records\\n\\nMuscatine Migrant Committee records\\n\\nMujeres Latinas Oral Histories: Juanita and Ernest Rodriguez, 2005; Ernest Rodriguez, 2006; Estefania Rodriguez, 2005\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"c847e8e98a3d58b228be795f49c127e0\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:32:49','2022-04-24 19:32:50','2022-04-24 19:32:49',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1552,1,14,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_d7ee7bfb44729dbeedb334f6d1403e87\",\"label\":\"Biographical / Historical\",\"type\":\"bioghist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Civil rights activist Ernest Rodriguez was born in 1928 in the predominantly Mexican settlement in Bettendorf known as Holy City. His father, Norberto Rodriguez, was born in Arandas in the state of Jalisco, Mexico, and migrated to the United States in 1910 at the age of seventeen. He met Muggie Belva Adams, an African American woman from Alabama, in the coal mining community of Lovilia, Iowa. They married in 1919 and stayed in south central Iowa, living in coal mining towns such as Albia, Centerville, and Buxton. In 1923, they moved to Bettendorf, Iowa, after Norberto Rodriguez secured work in the foundry of the Bettendorf Company. \\n\\nThe Rodriguez family lived in Holy City\'s boxcars and \\\"flats\\\" from 1924 to 1937 when they moved to southwest Davenport. Growing up with nine siblings, Rodriguez remembers \\\"onion topping\\\" in the nearby fields of Pleasant Valley, his mother feeding hobos during the Great Depression, as well as discriminatory practices his family encountered when they moved \\\"uptown.\\\" Ernest Rodriguez graduated from Davenport High School (now known as Davenport Central High School) in 1946 and served in the U.S. Army from 1951 to 1953 when he was stationed in El Paso, Texas.\\n\\nIn 1955, he married Juanita Serrano Segobiano, who pursued the path of a homemaker and chief caretaker of their nine children: Philip, Rebecca, Carlos, Christine, Maria, Yolanda, Ernest Jr., Alfonso, and Martin. Ernest Rodriguez was a member of the Amalgamated Meat Cutters and Butcher Workmen (AMCBW) Local 431 at the Oscar Mayer packinghouse in Davenport where he worked from 1959 to 1970. This period coincides with his increasing political and civil rights activism and his involvement as a founding member of local chapters of two Mexican American organizations - the American GI Forum and the League of United Latin American Citizens (LULAC Council 10). Through these organizations, Rodriguez worked with other Davenport activists to push forward a strong civil rights agenda beginning in the late 1950s and continuing throughout his life.\\n\\nDuring the 1960s, he served on the Davenport Human Relations Commission where he successfully targeted the need for a paid, full-time director to the commission. He stressed the importance of improving police-community relations as well as the need for the Davenport Community School District to hire more minority teachers, including Spanish-speaking teachers. In the late 1960s, Rodriguez served as co-chair of the Quad City Grape Boycott Committee to support the Delano, California, grape strike led by Cesar Chavez and Dolores Huerta.\\n\\nIn 1970, as director of the newly created Area Board for Migrants, Rodriguez was instrumental in lobbying the state legislature for the creation of the Spanish Speaking Peoples Commission - the forerunner of the Iowa Commission on Latino Affairs. He advocated for a better understanding of Latino rights in Iowa and across the country. During the 1960s and 1970s, he initiated two bilingual newspapers (El Reportero and Columnas) as well as educational radio and television programs that reached a wide audience. Rodriguez worked as an Equal Employment Officer at the Rock Island Arsenal from 1975 until his retirement in 1990 after which he continued to serve on civil rights organizations including the Illinois Migrant Council and the Davenport Civil Rights Commission. In 2018, Rodriguez was inducted into the Iowa Latino Hall of Fame for his lifelong advocacy for social justice.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"68193bd31d2e7ca48876aab04a8ec8db\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:32:50','2022-04-24 19:32:50','2022-04-24 19:32:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1553,1,14,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_ff7536d284c46a17a801e3c600c49f13\",\"label\":\"Scope and Contents\",\"type\":\"scopecontent\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The Ernest Rodriguez papers date from 1910 to 2014 and measure 10.5 linear inches. The papers were donated as part of the Mujeres Latinas Project, a project of the Iowa Women\'s Archives to preserve family and community history of Iowa Latinas and Latinos. The papers are organized in six series: Biographical information (1910-2005); Newspaper clippings (1968-2006); Organizations (1959-2007); Writings and publications (1968-2007); Oral history transcripts (1990\'s);and Associated printed materials (1957-1997).\\n\\nThe Biographical information series (1910-2005) includes a copy of Rodriguez\'s birth certificate, which states his birth as \\\"Box Car #8\\\" in Bettendorf, Iowa. The boxcars were located in the predominately Mexican settlement known as Holy City on the Bettendorf riverfront south of State Street and extending east of the former Bettendorf Shops for approximately one mile. A \\\"tank farm\\\" now occupies this area. The series includes his father\'s 1910 immigration card and later correspondence with immigration and naturalization officials. A computer-generated map of Holy City is included as well as the history of the Macias brothers, who came to Holy City during the First World War. This series also includes certificates and awards presented to Rodriguez.\\n\\nThe Newspaper clippings series (1968-2006) includes articles about Rodriguez\'s role in civil rights work in the Quad Cities area from the implementation of police seminars in the 1960s to his work as the Rock Island Arsenal\'s first Spanish-speaking program coordinator. Later clippings include reports of awards for his civil rights activism and an article about the death of his nephew, Marine Sgt. Norbert Gene Simmons in Da Nang, South Vietnam in 1966.\\n\\nThe Organizations series (1959-2007) reflects the wide range of community and social justice activities undertaken by Ernest Rodriguez. The LULAC Council 10 materials consist of histories and anniversary celebrations, plus a Latino guide to the Quad Cities. Included is information about the Chicano Educational Productions, a non-profit organization directed by Rodriguez, which broadcast a bilingual educational television program in the 1970s, a PowerPoint presentation for LULAC Council 10\'s fiftieth anniversary celebration banquet, \\\"The first ten years 1959-1969\\\" from 2009, and national LULAC: \\\"Our Legacy: The first fifty years 1929-1979\\\", 1979.\\n\\nThe Writings and publications series (1968-2007) contains Rodriguez\'s presentations, speeches, letters to the editor, radio scripts, histories, memoirs, and Lucas Castillo\'s life story by Ernest Rodriguez (1993). Topics in the radio broadcast scripts for \\\"Viewpoint\\\" range from migrant worker rights in Iowa to the1976 passage of the bill to establish the Iowa Spanish Speaking Peoples Commission - signed into law by Iowa Governor Robert Ray. The speeches were written to address groups ranging from University of Iowa law students to Mexican Americans in Fort Madison, Iowa. The writings in \\\"Impressions\\\" have been kept in their original binder; they include papers he wrote on civil rights in the Midwest and the Southwest, the emergence of the Chicano movement, and the role of the Catholic Church in promoting social justice, as well as family histories.\\n\\nThe oral history transcripts series includes seven interviews with immigrants who came from Mexico at the beginnin gof the 20th century and settled in the Quad Cities, which were conducted in the 1990s by Ernest Rodriguez and Grace Núñez; and a list of Mexican immigrants who settled in Davenport/Bettendorf area. The associated printed materials series includes a traditional Mexican recipe book, a book on the history of media portrayal of Chicanos, African Americans and American Indians, along with the several other samples of Rodriguez\'s reading interests.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"9141b69410922377c79ae8a1339570b2\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:32:50','2022-04-24 19:32:50','2022-04-24 19:32:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1554,1,14,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"label\":\"Acknowledgment\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This record was shared by the Iowa Women\'s Archives, the University of Iowa Libraries as a part of the ArchivesSpace Inclusive Examples project. For more information, reach out to the institution.\",\"publish\":false,\"subnote_guid\":\"057b48a61e35896afe00678ec219d9eb\"}],\"persistent_id\":\"825fb02401a75277a2a888fb85646547\"}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:32:50','2022-04-24 19:32:50','2022-04-24 19:32:50',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1565,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_7633c6713136ee465a1965d7031e9728\",\"type\":\"physloc\",\"content\":[\"For current information on the location of these materials, please consult the Penn State University Libraries catalog via the link above. Archival collections may be housed in offsite storage. For materials stored offsite, please allow 2-3 business days for retrieval.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1566,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_ea0e1b61d79b1a141e8365d6725219e5\",\"type\":\"abstract\",\"content\":[\"Luis Alberto Sánchez era un escritor peruano prominente y lider politico del Alianza Popular Revolucionaria Americana (APRA). Se afilio en 1931 con APRA y era encarcelado y exiliado por su activismo. Ultimamente, el era senador peruano, rector de el Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos de Lima, presidente del Senado y vicepresidente de Peru. Tambien era un educador y ensenaba a traves de latinoamerica y de los Estados Unidos. Esta coleccion de correspondencia, manuscritos y otro material documenta su liderazgo en la Alianza Popular Revolucionaria Americana (APRA), relacion con Victor Raul Haya de la Torre, la historia politica de Peru, movimientos politicos latinoamericanos en la mitad del siglo 20 y sus relaciones con escritores latinoamericanos prominentes. Estos documentos tambien incluyen su coleccion de la correspondencia de Alfredo Gonza&amp;#769;lez Prada, otro escritor y diplomatico peruano.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1567,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_29e2c510777cf4df620054f3e1b35969\",\"label\":\"Copyright Notice\",\"type\":\"userestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Copias fotostáticas de documentos originales pueden ser proveído para propósitos investigatorios por el criterio del Eberly Family Special Collections Library. Copias fotostáticas o reproducciones de documentos originales pueden ser sometido a tarifas describido en la política reproducción de Pennsylvania State University Libraries. Propietario de derechos intelectuales es retenido por los autores de artículos en estes papeles, o sus descendientes, como estupilado por los leyes de copyright estadounidense. Investigadores buscando un consejo sobre la disponibilidad de artículos no publicado para publicación deben consultar pertinient leyes de copyright y libelo.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"a7fab0ab6e418cbb3c171f9a1615f199\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1568,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_7b01fde73130147fceaff71073127301\",\"label\":\"Preferred Citation\",\"type\":\"prefercite\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"[Identificación de artíuclo], Luis Alberto Sánchez papers, 01764, Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"865bcd73a5c52a46cdd8c256ff47df44\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1569,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_062312e9f3f5649a1f918822c2ccdc0d\",\"label\":\"Access Restrictions\",\"type\":\"accessrestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Colección está abierta para investigaciones\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"cfd610f0ea5d8d8a68f6c9397bcb9863\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1570,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_fb2d9a9cdf9648f639fa3a53306dd2fd\",\"label\":\"Related Materials\",\"type\":\"relatedmaterial\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party (Peru) collection (Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University)\\n\\nArchivo Luis Alberto Sánchez (Biblioteca Nacional de Perú)\\n\\nMagda Portal papers (Nettie Lee Benson Latin American Collection, Teresa Lozano Long Institute for Latin American Studies (LLILAS), University of Texas at Austin)\\n\\n<title>Literature and Politics in Latin America: An Annotated Calendar of the Luis Alberto Sanchez Correspondence, 1919-1980</title> (Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University)\\n\\nSe puede encontrar libros del biblioteca de Luis Alberto Sánchez buscando con el expresión \\\"library of Luis Alberto Sánchez\\\" en el catálogo\\n\\nSe puede encontrar folletos relacionados al Alianza Popular Revolucionaria Americana (APRA) buscando con el expresión \\\"APRA, a collection of political pamphlets\\\" en el catálogo\\n\\nUna versión de este quía es disponible en inglés por este <a href=\\\"https://libraries.psu.edu/findingaids/1764en.htm\\\">vínculo</a>.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"df902730e18377f396849919353ae51a\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1571,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_e9704ea3b46c90bf90e52f14ac59c8ba\",\"label\":\"Scope and Contents\",\"type\":\"scopecontent\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Los documentos de Luis Alberto Sánchez documentan el profesión de Luis Alberto Sánchez como litetario destacado, político peruano y líder del Alianza Popular Revolucionaria Americana (APRA). Esta colección esta ordenado en seis series: <title>Alianza Popular Revolucionaria Americana</title>, <title>Correspondencia</title>, <title>Manuscritos y publicaciones</title>, <title>Alfredo González Prada</title>, <title>Material coleccionado</title> y <title>Penn State University</title>. Las series <title>Alianza Popular Revolucionaria Americana</title>, <title>Correspondencia</title>, <title>Manuscritos y publicaciones</title> y <title>Alfredo González Prada</title> principalmente están ordenados al nivel del documento. Mayormente esta colección consiste de correspondencia, manuscritos y publicaciones. Esta coleción también incluye recortes, propaganda político y manuscritos por otros escritores latinoamericanos.\\n\\nEsta colección demuestra el red social superpuesto entre literarios y políticos izquierdistas en la mitad del siglo 20. También da entendimiento al impacto de eventos internacionales en latinoamerica, inculido al Segundo Guerra Mundial y la Guerra Fría. La corespondencia, materiales políticos y manuscritos son importantes para intender la historia política de Perú, el desarrollo y manejo de APRA y los relaciones entre apristas durante periodos de exilio y persecución político. Esta colección incluye recuentos de comunismo, exilio, golpes de estado, suicidio y violencia político.\\n\\nLos papeles de Luis Alberto Sánchez incluye una cantidad abundante de corespondencia escrito por el fundador de APRA Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre, líderes de APRA como Felipe Cossío del Pomar y Manuel Seoane, y corespondencia por otras apristas. Notablamente ausente de esta colección es correspondencia entre dr. Sánchez y fundadora y anterior líder de APRA Magda Portal. La correspondencia y estritos incluye otras figuras políticos en paises como Colombia, la República Dominicana, Guatemala y Panama, incluido ex-presidentes y activistas como Jorge Mañach, Gabriel del Mazo y Juan Natalicio González.\\n\\nEsta colección también inculye el correspondencia personal de Alfredo González Prada, un escritor y diplomático prominente peruano quien era amigos con Luis Alberto Sánchez\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"4d4ce6c1ee871472df164837c6f59f27\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1572,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_4bbf6dcf23d6154161ae9b4af1205501\",\"label\":\"Custodial History\",\"type\":\"custodhist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Luis Alberto Sánchez creaba y acumulaba estes documentos. Los documentos sobrevivía periodos de exilio y viviendo internacionalmente. En 1969, Luis Alberto Sánchez vendió un porción de su biblioteca y sus papeles a Pennsylvania State University. Pennsylvania State University recibió esta compra inicial en tres cargamentos entre 1970 y 1974. Luis Alberto Sánchez vendió otro porción de su biblioteca a Pennsylvania State Unversity en el año 1997 que incluyó su colección Alfredo González Prada. Pennsylvania State University recibió otros acumulaciones mas pequeños de materiales hasta el fallecimiento de Luis Alberto Sánchez, incluyendo unas carpetas de correspondencia de la embajada peruana en 1991.\\n\\nLa historia de la custodia de la correspondencia de Alfredo González Prada no es conocido. Luis Alberto Sánchez era un amigo de Alfredo González Prada y un corresponsal de sus familiares. En algún momento, Luis Alberto Sánchez pasé de ser dueño de la correspondencia, manuscritos y otras materiales poseído por Alfredo González Prada. Luis Alberto Sánchez donaba parte de su colección al Biblioteca Nacional de Perú y ventió el resto a Pennsylvania State University.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"fc281e57ba776808046d06f90e186f65\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1573,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_b120f8336f6578fc1907f14b48b4b431\",\"label\":\"Processing Information\",\"type\":\"processinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"No que conocido quien empaquetaba y enviaba los primeros cargamentos de documentos y libros a Pennsylvania State University. Luis Alberto Sánchez contrató a Frida Parnes de Sardón para empaquetar y enviar el cargamento en 1977. Después de ser traslado a Pennsylvania State University empleados de colecciones especiales incluyendo conservador Don Henderson y bibliotecario Grace Pérez procesaban los documentos. Don Henderson trabajaba con Luis Alberto Sánchez para ordenar la correspondencia e identificar cartas escritos usando seudónimos. En algún momento, un empleado oredenaba e indexaba los artículos del <title>Cuaderno de bitácora</title>. Pennsylvania State University se guardaba en microfilm la mayoría de la correspondencia y artículos, pero más correspondencia era añadido después de éste proyecto. Empleados de colecciones especiales procesaba y ordenaba esta colección varios veces y cualquier orden original ha sido perdido. En algún momento empleados de colecciones especiales interarchivaban material acumulado por colecciones especiales sobre Luis Alberto Sánchez, la colección y otras colecciones relacionado a la historia de latinoamerica en este colección. \\n\\nEn 2019, Lexy deGraffenreid reprocesó la colección para facilitar mejor acceso y uso. Ella reprocessó la mayoría de la colección en el nivel del artículo pero retuvo el orden físico imponido por Don Henderson, Grace Pérez y empleados de colecciones especiales. En consultación con conservadora Ángel Díaz, se retuvo la mayoría de material acumulado por Pennsylvania State University como parte de los documentos, incluyendo la correspondencia original entre Luis Alberto Sánchez y Pennsylvania State University. Ella quitó acuerdos originales, el índice artículos del <title>Cuaderno de bitácora</title> y materiales relacionado a otras colecciones latinoamericanos al carpeta del colección. También devolvió guardados de microfilm de la correspondencia y artículos a la colección. Lexy deGraffenreid ordenó intelectualmente la colección en seis series para facilitar el entendimiento de investigadores sobre el colección y recalcar varios grupos de documentos (como la correspnodencia de Alfredo González Prada).\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"8ac70bcbb9c43ba4809b9456f7549d14\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1574,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_58a1a0d98d4f23f0dc6d7584c51f1e63\",\"label\":\"Biographical / Historical\",\"type\":\"bioghist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Luis Alberto Sánchez (12 de octubre de 1900 a 6 de febrero de 1994) era un escritor, político y líder del Alianza Popular Revolucionaria Americana (APRA). Nació en Lima en 1900, ingresó al Colegio Sagrados Corazones Recoleta en 1908 y al Facultad de Letras de la Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos en 1917. Ingresó en el Partido Aprista Peruano (PAP) en 1931 y fue encarcelado debajo el goberino de Luis Miguel Sánchez Cerro. Sánchez salió de Perú como un exiliado y entre 1931 y 1960 sobrevivía periodos de exilio y persecución político por gobiernos peruanos. Durante este tiempo, se fundaba el periódico aprista <title>La Tribuna</title> y viajaba para instruir en varios paises, incluyendo Cuba, Guatemala, México, Puerto Rico y los Estados Unidos. En los 1960s, fue eligido Senador del Departmento de Lima (1963-1969), elegido Rector de la Universidad de San Marcos de Lima, sirvió como presidente del Senado y fue presidente de la 52a Asamblea de la Unión Parlamentaria Internacional. Después del golpe de estado en 1968, fue detenido por el gobierno de Juan Velasco Alvarado. Sánchez se continuó su trabajo literario y político en los 1970s y 1980s. Últimamente fue elejido senador por Lima (1980-1985) y vicepresidente (1985-1990). Era casado con Mercedes Vargas Quintanilla y Rosa Dergán. También ha tenido seis hijos.\\n\\nAlfredo González Prada (16 de octubre de 1891 a 27 de junio 2 1943) era un escritor, diplomático e hijo de escritores Adriana de Verneuil de González Prada y Manuel González Prada. Debido a su carrera político, vivió la mayoría de su video afuera del Perú. Servió en la embajada peruana en Washington D.C. y fue presidente de la delagación peruana a la Liga de las Naciones. Casó con estadounidense Elizabeth Anne How y se suicidó en 1943.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"f2333a9a1ea2576f6eead9c67cda4ece\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1575,1,18,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"label\":\"Acknowledgment\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This record was shared by the Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University as a part of the ArchivesSpace Inclusive Examples project. For more information, reach out to the institution.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"7e031ee7f5e795056f73bcacaaef976c\"}],\"persistent_id\":\"d57df67ff2054e0bf095d31d44c4a7bb\"}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05','2022-04-24 19:36:05',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1576,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_027d50cbe2f592222b2d3e6f660b4b8c\",\"type\":\"physloc\",\"content\":[\"For current information on the location of these materials, please consult the Penn State University Libraries catalog via the link above. Archival collections may be housed in offsite storage. For materials stored offsite, please allow 2-3 business days for retrieval.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1577,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_a203ed8644eeb62aab9e226fe9b79a1c\",\"type\":\"abstract\",\"content\":[\"Luis Alberto Sanchez was a prominent Peruvian author and political leader of the American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party (APRA). He joined APRA in 1931 and, he was jailed and exiled for his activism. Ultimately, he became a Peruvian senator, Rector of the Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos, President of the Senate, and Vice President of Peru. He was also an educator and taught throughout Latin America and the United States. This collection of correspondence, manuscripts, and other materials documents his leadership in the American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party (APRA), relationship with Victor Raul Haya de la Torre, Peruvian political history, mid-20th century Latin American political movements, and relationships with prominent Latin American authors. These papers also include his collection of Alfredo Gonzalez Prada correspondence, another prominent Peruvian diplomat and writer.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1578,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_bd716a6c1a331c631ff3f4641b41e6a4\",\"label\":\"Conditions Governing Access\",\"type\":\"accessrestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This collection is open for research.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"891fd15b86d013516f742b2dc6f5fddc\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1579,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_140d2b277e6d119ad95be7259364cd4b\",\"label\":\"Scope and Contents\",\"type\":\"scopecontent\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The Luis Alberto Sánchez papers documents Luis Alberto Sánchez\' career as both a prominent literary figure, Peruvian politician, and leader in the American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party (APRA). The collection is arranged into six series: <title>American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party (APRA)</title>, <title>Correspondence</title>, <title>Manuscripts and Publications</title>, <title>Alfredo González Prada</title>, <title>Collected Materials</title>, and <title>Pennsylvania State University</title>. The <title>American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party (APRA)</title>, <title>Correspondence</title>, <title>Manuscripts and Publications</title>, and <title>Alfredo González Prada</title> series are chiefly arranged at the item level. The collection chiefly consists of Sánchez\' correspondence, manuscripts, and publications. This collection also includes clippings, political propaganda, and writings by other Latin American literary figures.\\n\\nThis collection demonstrates the overlapping networks of left-leaning political movements and literary circles in the mid-20th century. It also provides insight into the impact of international events on Latin America, including World War II and the beginnings of the Cold War. The correspondence, political materials, and manuscripts in collection are important towards understanding Peruvian political history, the development and management of APRA, and the relationships maintained between Apristas during periods of exile and political persecution. This collection includes accounts of communism, coups, exile, political violence, and suicide.\\n\\nThe Luis Alberto Sanchez papers includes a substantial amount of correspondence written by APRA founder Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre, APRA leaders such as Felipe Cossío del Pomar and Manuel Seoane, and other Apristas. Notably absent from this collection is correspondence between Dr. Sánchez and APRA co-founder and former APRA leader Magda Portal. The correspondence features other Latin American political figures in countries such as Colombia, Dominican Republic, Guatemala, and Panama, including former presidents and political activists such as Jorge Mañach, Gabriel del Mazo, and Juan Natalicio González.\\n\\nThis collection also includes the personal correspondence of Alfredo González Prada, a prominent Peruvian author and diplomat who was friends with Luis Alberto Sánchez.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"670b8df437b336e077fb7d75dd05d316\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1580,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_0a816e3c88b8c5bfaf2ac3c1ec397f16\",\"label\":\"Custodial History\",\"type\":\"custodhist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Luis Alberto Sánchez created and accumulated the materials in this collection. These materials survived periods of exile and living internationally. In 1969, Luis Alberto Sánchez sold a portion of his library collection and his papers to Pennsylvania State University. Pennsylvania State University received this initial purchase in three shipments between 1970-1974. Luis Alberto Sánchez sold an additional portion of his library to Pennsylvania State University in 1977 which included the Alfredo González Prada materials. Pennsylvania State University received several smaller accruals of material until Dr. Sánchez\' death, including several folders of correspondence from the Peruvian embassy in 1991. \\n\\nThe custodial history of the Alfredo González Prada correspondence is unknown. Luis Alberto Sánchez was a friend of Alfredo González Prada and correspondent with his family. At some point, Luis Alberto Sánchez came into possession of correspondence, manuscripts, and other material owned by Alfredo González Prada. Dr. Sánchez donated some of this collection to the Biblioteca Nacional de Perú and sold the remainder to Pennsylvania State University along with his papers.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"ad2523dabe427aa05596fb234c006330\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1581,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_2e6a7b5eb5741021a1bab68b34625094\",\"label\":\"Processing Information\",\"type\":\"processinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"It is unknown who packaged and shipped the original shipments of materials to Pennsylvania State University. Dr. Sánchez hired Frida Parnes de Sardón to package and ship the 1977 shipment. Once transferred to Pennsylvania State University, Special Collections staff processed the materials, including curator Don Henderson and librarian Grace Pérez. Don Henderson collaborated with Luis Alberto Sánchez to arrange the correspondence and identify letters written under pseudonyms. At some point, an individual arranged and indexed the <title>Cuaderno de Bitácora</title> articles. Pennsylvania State University microfilmed the majority of the correspondence and <title>Cuaderno de Bitácora</title> articles, however additional correspondence was added to the collection after microfilming. Special Collections staff processed and arranged this collection multiple times and any original order has been lost. At some point Special Collections staff interfiled material collected by Special Collections staff about Luis Alberto Sánchez, the collection, and other Latin American history collections at Pennsylvania State University into the collection.\\n\\nIn 2019, Lexy deGraffenreid reprocessed this collection to facilitate better access and use. She reprocessed the majority of the collection at the item level, but retained the original physical order created by Don Henderson, Grace Pérez, and special collections staff. In consultation with curator Angel Diaz, she retained most of the Pennsylvania State University collected material in the collection, including the original correspondence between Dr. Sánchez and Pennsylvania State University. She removed original agreements, the <title>Cuaderno de Bitácora</title> index, and materials relating to other Latin American collections to the Collection Folder. She also reconstituted the master microfilm reels of collection material into the collection. Lexy deGraffenreid intellectually arranged the collection into six series to facilitate researcher understanding of the collection and to highlight different groupings of material (such as the Alfredo González Prada correspondence). Lexy deGraffenreid arranged and inventoried this collection in Spanish. She did not translate the inventory into English for the English-language finding aid.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"34a787c4204145d8f6cdeb2579066a88\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:52','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1582,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_9c1d7739a82258ca7169408b83a6df89\",\"label\":\"Biographical / Historical\",\"type\":\"bioghist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Luis Alberto Sánchez (October 12, 1900-February 6, 1994) was an author, politician, and leader of the American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party (APRA). He was born in Lima in 1900 and entered the Colegio Sagrados Corazones Recoleta in 1908 and the Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos in 1917. He joined the Partido Aprista Peruano (PAP) in 1931 and was imprisoned under Luis Miguel Sánchez Cerro. Sanchez went into exile in 1932 and through 1960 endured periods of exile and political persecution by Peruvian governments. During this time, he founded the APRA party newspaper <title>La Tribuna</title>, and travelled to teach in many countries, including Cuba, Guatemala, Mexico, and the United States (including Puerto Rico). In the 1960s, he was elected Senator of the Department of Lima (1963-1969), elected Rector of the Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos de Lima, served as President of the Senate, and was President of the 52nd Asamblea de la Unión Parlamentaria Internacional. Following the 1968 coup, he was detained by the Juan Velasco Alvarado government. Dr. Sánchez continued his literary and political work throughout the 1970s and 1980s, and was ultimately elected Senator for Lima (1980-1985) and Vice President (1985-1990). He was married to Mercedes Vargas Quintanilla and Rosa Dergán and had six children.\\n\\nAlfredo González Prada (October 16, 1891-June 27, 1943) was an author, diplomat, and son of writers Adriana de Verneuil de González Prada and Manuel González Prada. Due to his political career, he lived most of his life outside of Perú. He served in the Peruvian Embassy in Washington D.C. and was president of the Peruvian delegation to the League of Nations. He married the American Elizabeth Anne Howe and died by suicide in 1943.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"b24e3bc7253139087aa94d2875f7cba1\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1583,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_cd0d923e1e41afed294f99eccc85c432\",\"label\":\"Conditions Governing Use\",\"type\":\"userestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Photocopies of original materials may be made available for research purposes at the discretion of the Eberly Family Special Collections Library. Photocopies or reproductions of original materials may be subject to fees as outlined by the Pennsylvania State University Libraries reproduction policies. Copyright is retained by the creators of items in these papers, or their descendants, as stipulated by United States copyright law. Patrons seeking advice on the availability of unpublished materials for publication should consult relevant copyright law and laws of libel.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"637e0083b453b2f96ab89146c7b8c6b3\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1584,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_5da40fcb9a9a88a7260b2f354bd9ba4e\",\"label\":\"Preferred Citation\",\"type\":\"prefercite\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"[Identification of item], Luis Alberto Sánchez papers, 01764, Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"bd8c8de628e729ae8463b7e832e1dc66\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1585,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_756775a6b0e438821c9685135e5fe589\",\"label\":\"Related Materials\",\"type\":\"relatedmaterial\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party (Peru) collection (Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University)\\n\\nArchivo Luis Alberto Sánchez (Biblioteca Nacional de Perú)\\n\\nMagda Portal papers (Nettie Lee Benson Latin American Collection, Teresa Lozano Long Institute for Latin American Studies (LLILAS), University of Texas at Austin)\\n\\n<title>Literature and Politics in Latin America: An Annotated Calendar of the Luis Alberto Sanchez Correspondence, 1919-1980</title> (Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University)\\n\\nBooks from the library of Luis Alberto Sánchez located in Pennsylvania State University Libraries can be found by searching the library catalog using the phrase \\\"library of Luis Alberto Sánchez\\\".\\n\\nPamphlets related to the American Popular Revolutionary Alliance Party can be found by searching the library catalog using the phrase \\\"APRA, a collection of political pamphlets\\\"\\n\\nA Spanish version of this finding aid is available at the following <a href=\\\"https://libraries.psu.edu/findingaids/1764es.htm\\\">link</a>.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"cb266a70f14478e93737af7d5cd7690d\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1586,1,19,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"label\":\"Acknowledgment\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This record was shared by the Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University as a part of the ArchivesSpace Inclusive Examples project. For more information, reach out to the institution.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"4ffeb47ef360863c47e5d3d34b6587e7\"}],\"persistent_id\":\"175bdc7e1c9b4f547f4d76cd883f468c\"}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53','2022-04-24 19:36:53',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1609,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_87d0874f9564a0d9d2df919d0ebec9fb\",\"type\":\"abstract\",\"content\":[\"The Richard Saholt Collection contains materials created by and about Richard Saholt (1924–2014), a Minneapolis based collage artist. The collection comprises correspondence, medical and military files, artwork reproductions, audiovisual materials, print and slide photography, a collaged artist\'s book, a scrapbook, and press materials related to Saholt\'s life and work. The collection was donated by Michael Bonesteel after he gifted collages accessioned into the American Folk Art Museum\'s artwork collection. The materials exemplify Saholt\'s artistic process, revealing traumatic biographical events and motivations that informed the imagery in his work. The collection may be of interest to researchers studying self-taught art, outsider art, artists working with mental illnesses, artist veterans, collage, artists\' books, military history, and Minneapolis-based artists. The materials within the collection range from 1922 to 2011.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1610,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_32ed73142168f08d39b641287237f680\",\"label\":\"Conditions Governing Access\",\"type\":\"accessrestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The collection is open for research. Medical documents are restricted.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"6adc3b057168c307c985c18929ed08af\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1611,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_35525700d45769604d84c32757c1f063\",\"label\":\"Immediate Source of Acquisition\",\"type\":\"acqinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The collection was donated by Michael Bonesteel in 2021.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"69bd332a36d220f4eddb171e97cb39ec\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1612,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_11ba93542f8c91c9fdd178d04fd06fb0\",\"label\":\"Arrangement\",\"type\":\"arrangement\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The collection is arranged by type of materials and then alphabetically by subject. Box 1 contains textual materials including correspondence, exhibition catalogs, brochures, and press clippings. The folders are arranged alphabetically by subject and the files are arranged chronologically within each folder. Box 2 consists of graphic materials: printed collage reproductions, artwork and exhibition photography, family and personal photographs, slides, and business/membership cards. Box 3 houses three VHS cassettes in collaged covers and an annotated copy of the Principles of General Psychology. Box 4 contains oversized artwork print reproductions, a collaged artist book, and a scrapbook.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"f1624a3699011f020950afbcd5e2713c\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1613,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_3f5c258f102631104b9456846b01740a\",\"label\":\"Biographical / Historical\",\"type\":\"bioghist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Richard \\\"Dick\\\" Louis Saholt was born on February 22, 1924, in Minneapolis, Minnesota to Torfen F. and Josemone R. Saholt. Saholt experienced a traumatic childhood, marked by physical and emotional abuse from his father, Torfen. As a child, Saholt suffered undiagnosed learning disabilities and a speech impediment. He was described as a gentle, sensitive, and shy child. \\n\\n<br/> In December of 1942, at the age of 17, Saholt enlisted in the U.S. Army to get away from his bad home life. Saholt served as a sniper, scout, and point man in the Ski Paratroopers of the 10th Mountain Division. The 10th Mountain Division fought and defeated German forces during World War II in the Italian Alps between 1944 and 1945. On November 5, 1945, the military discharged Saholt with back and leg injuries. The military awarded him a Good Conduct Medal, Combat Infantry Badge, and a Bronze Star. Saholt returned from combat with physical injuries, post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), visual disturbances, depression, and blackouts. His childhood speech impediment also returned. To overcome his stutter, he enrolled in singing lessons where he met his future wife Doris Marie Johnson. The couple married in 1952. \\n\\n<br/> Following the war, Saholt\'s physical and psychological ailments continued to grow, impairing his ability to complete training programs or hold a job for more than a few months. For 29 years, Saholt repeatedly appealed to the Veterans Administration (VA) for service-connected disability. During an early appeal in 1964, the head of the Minneapolis Veterans of Foreign Wars office encouraged Saholt to create collages to visualize what he struggled to explain through words. Saholt began to document his experience using war mementos, words, and phrases clipped from newspapers. These collages helped him to articulate the psychological traumas caused by the war. \\n\\n<br/> The VA continued to discredit his claims. It denied the impact of the war on his disability and blamed his childhood for his psychological ailments. In 1969, Saholt\'s attorney drew medical files out of the VA, revealing Saholt had been diagnosed with \\\"chronic undifferentiated schizophrenia,\\\" a diagnosis kept hidden from him. In 1974, a representative from the Disabled American Veterans organization, used the unearthed file to appeal his case which finally awarded him service-connected disability. \\n\\n<br/> Saholt continued to create collages and expanded the size of his work to large-scale montages constructed using photographs and clippings from journals, magazines, and newspapers. By the mid-1990s, Saholt built an impressive body of work encompassing hundreds of collages. He began to share his work in group exhibitions and publications. In 2001, the American Visionary Art Museum in Baltimore included his work in a group exhibition. Additionally, he was featured in solo exhibitions at the University of Wisconsin-Stout in 2005 and the Intuit: Center for Intuitive and Outsider Art in 2016. \\n\\n<br/> Saholt passed away at the age of 89 on January 12, 2014. Today, Saholt\'s work is included in several museum collections, including the American Folk Art Museum, New York, NY; Inuit: The Center for Intuitive and Outsider Art, Chicago, IL; National Veterans Art Museum, Chicago, IL; and the National Alliance on Mental Illness, St. Paul, MN. \\n\\n<br/> Sources:\\n\\n Aronson, Virginia. \\\"A Dream Come Alive: The Collages of Richard Saholt,\\\" Raw Vision, Summer 1994: 44-47. <br/> Saholt, Richard, subject. \\\"The Life and Art of Richard Saholt,\\\" Vimeo, uploaded by Living Spirit, March 2018. https://vimeo.com/259394425 <br/> Wolfe, Chante. \\\"A Soldier\'s Heart: Then and Now,\\\" Twin Cities Daily Planet, July 23, 2006. https://www.tcdailyplanet.net/soldiers-heart-then-and-now/\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"106970298241a70e06d859a3b4db138e\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1614,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_8e71dea0588a7641a84a5da2b2926fca\",\"label\":\"Physical Characteristics and Technical Requirements\",\"type\":\"phystech\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The three VHS video cassettes in the collection require a VCR and monitor to view.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"5ec3c8dcf5976dd48837d386a6be73f4\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1615,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_492222ba04662a1f4ca57e4012ece5c8\",\"label\":\"Preferred Citation\",\"type\":\"prefercite\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"[item description], [date], [collection name], box and folder numbers, American Folk Art Museum Archives, New York\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"3a0cea934701aa8ac4e81641a133fd6c\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1616,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_564b0403d280a850298ca2b06885dd7d\",\"label\":\"Processing Information\",\"type\":\"processinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The 35mm slides were removed from their original slide carousels and moved to polypropylene sleeves in their original order. Print photography was placed into PAT-approved sleeves and separated by type (exhibition photography, artwork, family photos). Paper clips were removed from correspondence, but not from the scrapbook as they are structurally and artistically part of the work. Packages and grouped letters are kept together within folders separated from other materials with acid-free interleaving paper. The VHS cassettes and their collaged covers are housed inside custom phase boxes.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"232c1dfa8e60dcf31fe5c182db83509e\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1617,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_a976a3aa2bdf141e6ee63c873e7a40e6\",\"label\":\"Related Materials\",\"type\":\"relatedmaterial\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Associated with object accession numbers 2016.1.1 - 2016.1.12\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"2bc1f87cee12da4ec8e79a2246620936\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1618,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_e6d64bc9c7068dd994f7f2749ba2671d\",\"label\":\"Scope and Contents\",\"type\":\"scopecontent\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The Richard Saholt Collection contains materials created by and about Richard Saholt, including correspondence, medical and military files, artwork reproductions, audiovisual materials, print and slide photography, a collaged artist\'s book, a scrapbook, and press materials related to Saholt\'s life and work. The collection was donated by Michael Bonesteel, a scholar and art historian, with whom Saholt began communication with in the 1990s. Following Saholt\'s death in 2014, Bonesteel inherited additional documents, correspondence, photography, videos, and books. The materials exemplify Saholt\'s artistic process, revealing the traumatic biographical events and motivations that informed the imagery in his work. The collection may be of interest to researchers studying self-taught art, outsider art, artists working with mental illnesses, artist veterans, collage, artists\' books, military history, and Minneapolis-based artists. The materials within the collection date from 1922 to 2011. \\n\\n<br/> The collection contains a significant amount of correspondence from Saholt to Michael Bonesteel from the mid-1990s through 2005. The correspondence comprises handwritten letters, facsimiles, annotated collage reproductions, and acknowledgments of artwork donations from Saholt to Bonesteel. These letters describe Saholt\'s desire to exhibit and publish his work and mention his interest in Bonesteel\'s scholarship on outsider art. While correspondence is primarily from Saholt to Michael Bonesteel, the collection does not contain Bonesteel\'s responses. Additional original handwritten letters and facsimiles document Saholt\'s ongoing legal battles with the Veterans Affairs Office requesting service-connected disability compensation. Correspondence relating to veterans affairs claims are housed within the same folder but pertain to various appeals made between 1945 and 2012. Correspondence and press packets from Saholt to journalists and publishers show additional intent to exhibit and publish his work. This folder contains both acceptance and rejection responses from writers and publishers. A small portion of the correspondence is letters from Saholt to family members including an endearing letter to his wife Doris. \\n\\n Additional correspondence encompasses psychological evaluations; records relating to his military history; annotated and collaged court hearings and testimonies; biographical material, medical, and military documentation on Torfen Saholt, Richard Saholt\'s father. Medical documentation and psychological evaluations are restricted. \\n\\n Photographic materials are undated and consist of personal family pictures and portraits of Saholt (from childhood through adult years), photography of exhibition installations and receptions, and reproductions of his collages in both print and 35mm slide format. Saholt\'s collage works are reproduced as color prints on paper, occasionally embellished with annotations and handwriting. \\n\\n Three VHS video cassettes, housed in collaged covers, feature interviews with the artist giving insight into his childhood and artistic process. Saholt inscribed, altered, and collaged three books in the collection. A copy of The Wall: Images and Offerings from the Vietnam Veterans Memorial is altered using collaged newspaper and magazine clippings to highlight the horrors of war and its traumatic effect on veterans. Saholt inscribed the interior cover of Principles of General Psychology, which depicts and discusses a collage created while under psychiatric care. The scrapbook combines visual and textual collages layered with family pictures, exhibition photography, and portraits of the artist. \\n\\n Press materials include newspapers, magazines, and exhibition catalogs related to Saholt\'s artwork and exhibitions published between 1985 and 2001. Additional newspaper clippings chronicle Saholt\'s publicized legal battles with the Veterans Affairs Office. A small collection of ephemera includes business and personal membership cards.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"e578633cedc817fc303a53291cef231c\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1619,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_8e1229822b7ae35e60704dea08ee2cfa\",\"label\":\"Conditions Governing Use\",\"type\":\"userestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The Richard Saholt collection is owned by the American Folk Art Museum. The collection is subject to all copyright laws, and is dedicated to public use for research, study and scholarship.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"c63ce5a8f584dca423026e0b9b722c92\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55','2022-04-24 19:42:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1620,1,3,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"label\":\"Acknowledgment\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This record was shared by the American Folk Art Museum as a part of the ArchivesSpace Inclusive Examples project. For more information, reach out to the institution.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"e55d7e6695cdbfd09c0e943b938b884d\"}],\"persistent_id\":\"a6c1bae84d7f54a08b67abc3a07621b8\"}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:42:56','2022-04-24 19:42:56','2022-04-24 19:42:56',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1657,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_langmaterial\",\"persistent_id\":\"6c4f56fed0d30bbd6c46edc67104555d\",\"type\":\"langmaterial\",\"content\":[\"English , German .\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,879,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1658,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_d290b6e5391c9a837218b1bffc706711\",\"label\":\"Condition Description\",\"type\":\"physdesc\",\"content\":[\"Loose leaves within the scrapbooks are brittle, as are the pamphlets enclosed in the larger binders.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1659,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_07f285178a650815cfe07a3b684d655d\",\"type\":\"physloc\",\"content\":[\"For current information on the location of these materials, please consult the Penn State University Libraries catalog via the link above. Archival collections may be housed in offsite storage. For materials stored offsite, please allow 2-3 business days for retrieval.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1660,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_ef23046c7ce5c527d8c3d4c4bdf7e14a\",\"type\":\"abstract\",\"content\":[\"This collection spans several decades of the \\\"golden age\\\" of tightlacing. Contents include main-stream media coverage of corseting issues, cross-dressing, and other associated subcultures; black-and-white photographs of corsetiers (presenting as masculine and/or feminine); illustrations and advertisements; a manuscript on the history of corsets; German publications on gender, including Das 3. Geschlecht (das Transvestiten) from the Weimar era; and a range of other ephemera. The creator took great pains to keep themselves anonymous but was likely a member of the tightlacing movement in some form.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1661,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_aa4ce90313d682e86d908fa91059d54c\",\"label\":\"Conditions Governing Access\",\"type\":\"accessrestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This collection is open for research.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"bb52a1eebf095546940b05fb6b8c9e0a\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1662,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_9897dd80173d1367d98eca0a377d6582\",\"label\":\"Conditions Governing Use\",\"type\":\"userestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Photocopies of original materials may be made available for research purposes at the discretion of the Eberly Family Special Collections Library. Photocopies or reproductions of original materials may be subject to fees as outlined by the Pennsylvania State University Libraries reproduction policies.\\n\\nCopyright is retained by the creators of items in these papers, or their descendants, as stipulated by United States copyright law. Patrons seeking advice on the availability of unpublished materials for publication should consult relevant copyright law and laws of libel.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"4637bc4474d21d2a3a997b31782f5c3a\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1663,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_7112c8e07699d3a428ed50881be3affd\",\"label\":\"Preferred Citation\",\"type\":\"prefercite\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"[Identification of Item], Early male tightlacing, corsetting, and cross-dressing collection, 10034, Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"027204bade176078044235a4c67e5e23\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1664,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_cc2738164a4b64e92f981ca1a9ed443d\",\"label\":\"Content Description\",\"type\":\"scopecontent\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This collection contains approximately 4 linear feet of scrapbooks and albums documenting six decades of corseting, tightlacing, and cross-dressing, among related topics and subcultures from 1880 through the end of the 1930s. The eight scrapbooks and two binders contain photographs, portraits, clippings, notes, drawings and a written manuscript by the anonymous creator. One of the binders also contains four copies of Das 3. Geschlecht (das Transvestiten), among other German-language ephemera from the Weimar era.\\n\\nThis collection also includes material featuring the Indigenous peoples of Papua New Guinea, traditional Indian piercings and dance, among other cultures, in an attempt to include as many \\\"tightlacing\\\" and \\\"crossdressing\\\" trends as possible. Researchers should note that these cultural practices are described using a Western-European cultural perspective and may be offensive or culturally sensitive to members of their originating communities.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"ebe800f0c2b11e7445376be1f4dec02d\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1665,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_5f74d488ca81b0d05b736b2dee791acb\",\"label\":\"Immediate Source of Acquisition\",\"type\":\"acqinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"These scrapbooks were purchased from Lux Mentis, Booksellers on 2020 March 21 using funds from the Malkin, Mann, and Espenshade fund lines.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"9786b3331934a5b9b378d4723569c577\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51','2022-04-24 19:50:51',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1666,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_84c63f0fea4a9df8370a0f6b3760c82d\",\"label\":\"Processing Information\",\"type\":\"processinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Gideon Goodrich minimally processed and described this material at the collection level by rehousing the scrapbooks in 13\\\" dropfront boxes (the materials arrived loose) and creating an inventory of the order they imposed on the collection. Gideon conducted additional research in order to describe two of the publications represented in this collection - <title>Facts and Fancies</title>, a small, subscription only publication, and <title>Recollections of a Corsetiere</title>, a section of <title>London Life</title> - in order to provide the most accurate dates and publication information possible.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"8b7e12ea9cb1196f083c9a5c8a2b0846\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1667,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_ed6e95e5d478a32eb623a0b05a354d4b\",\"label\":\"Related Materials\",\"type\":\"relatedmaterial\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Ethel Granger Archive of Photographs and Ephemera, #7772, Division of Rare and Manuscript Collections, Cornell University Library.\\n\\nPostcards of German female impersonators, #7636, Division of Rare and Manuscript Collections, Cornell University Library.\\n\\nNell Houston Brown Papers, 1883-1946, Tennessee State Library and Archives.\\n\\nCarroll Revue collection, MS 1983, Manuscripts and Archives Repository, Yale University Library. \\n\\nTransgender collection, MS 1848, Manuscripts and Archives Repository, Yale University Library.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"455a5d91cb5cf028f9bded8ead30c2f5\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1668,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_fedf75ddbf3261398b0a80284d8666fd\",\"label\":\"Appraisal\",\"type\":\"appraisal\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This scrapbook collection adds context and content to our LGBTIA+ collecting area, and also adds additional background on the Women\'s History and Experience area. While it documents male corset-wearers and high-heel aficionados, as well as female impersonators, it also contains content about female tightlacers and corsetieres as well. This may be some of our earliest material that relates to the experiences of trans and gender diverse people, which is worth noting. It is also worth noting that the scrapbooks appear to have been kept by someone who was actively involved in corset-wearing and tightlacing communities.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"0f765c7510230d38620e91db8d8fbbf0\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1669,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_5614c609a8317ca3caa47e078a49d1d9\",\"label\":\"Custodial History\",\"type\":\"custodhist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"These materials were collected and compiled by an unknown individual or individuals. The compete custodial history of this collection is unknown.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"506dd59472f043ee1a77373925446ad9\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1670,1,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"label\":\"Acknowledgment\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This record was shared by the Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University as a part of the ArchivesSpace Inclusive Examples project. For more information, reach out to the institution.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"1a477a3c035fdda0774e90b27af7fc48\"}],\"persistent_id\":\"d11494b8e4685aef50fabd498c74939f\"}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52','2022-04-24 19:50:52',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1729,1,NULL,NULL,22,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_digital_object\",\"persistent_id\":\"d32a8d44252c611535d91caa848d8c9c\",\"type\":\"note\",\"content\":[\"<p>Doughnut Production, circa early-mid 1960s</p>\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 23:35:01','2022-04-24 23:35:01','2022-04-24 23:35:01',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1730,1,NULL,NULL,26,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_digital_object\",\"persistent_id\":\"ae1c6796dee41bee38055da25a9dc42f\",\"type\":\"note\",\"content\":[\"<p>TV Commercial (2\\\" videotape): \\\"Krispy Kreme, n.d.\\\"</p>\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-24 23:35:30','2022-04-24 23:35:30','2022-04-24 23:35:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1734,1,NULL,NULL,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_digital_object\",\"persistent_id\":\"b5be14a5e576986ef01a0705aca79a49\",\"type\":\"note\",\"content\":[\"<p>Alabama: Mobile: Government Street [Cochran photos]</p>\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 02:44:42','2022-04-25 02:44:43','2022-04-25 02:44:42',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1748,1,23,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"type\":\"abstract\",\"content\":[\"This collection documents English Victorian musician and novelist Mary Mackay (more popularly known under her pseudonym Marie Corelli. Her works include themes relating to astral projection, psychic experiences, and mystical ideas associated with the Rosicrucianism spiritual and cultural movement. Her most famous work was (1895), which sold more than any previously published English novel. The collection includes correspondence, sheet music, photographs, scrapbooks, and other ephemera.\"],\"persistent_id\":\"23fb4f3afe33496286025d1eea36bfe5\"}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:07','2022-04-25 03:30:07','2022-04-25 03:30:07',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1761,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_2d4bfc9e08a76616cce118dc34cc9a6c\",\"type\":\"abstract\",\"content\":[\"This collection documents English Victorian musician and novelist Mary Mackay (more popularly known under her pseudonym Marie Corelli. Her works include themes relating to astral projection, psychic experiences, and mystical ideas associated with the Rosicrucianism spiritual and cultural movement. Her most famous work was <title>The Sorrows of Satan</title> (1895), which sold more than any previously published English novel. The collection includes correspondence, sheet music, photographs, scrapbooks, and other ephemera.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1762,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_singlepart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_5e4bd9831ac988f4e6dc027089ae918f\",\"type\":\"physloc\",\"content\":[\"For current information on the location of these materials, please consult the Penn State University Libraries catalog via the link above. Archival collections may be housed in offsite storage. For materials stored offsite, please allow 2-3 business days for retrieval.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1763,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_fbb51bc5b9dd98602891f676ca33bbe8\",\"label\":\"Access Restrictions\",\"type\":\"accessrestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This collection is open for access.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"55383c4a15632b9f337c11baa984d16a\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1764,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_e027072ace811b34462612a99f358e39\",\"label\":\"Immediate Source of Acquisition\",\"type\":\"acqinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The materials in box 1 and scrapbooks in Box 2 were purchased from David Mason Books in 2018. Additional boxes in Box 2 were purchased from Lord Durham Rare Books using the Milholland fund.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"96996fba46ca8f89c4bcf116e1a8c27d\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1765,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_90a42157920a9681ced4778eb67c8569\",\"label\":\"Biographical / Historical\",\"type\":\"bioghist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Mary Mackay (psuedonym Marie Corelli) was an English Victorian novelist born May 1, 1855 in London. Her parents were Elizabeth Mills and Charles Mackay, the Scottish journalist, novelist, and poet. She spent much of her childhood in a convent and started her career as a musician. She later became a writer, publishing her first novel, <title>A Romance of Two Worlds</title>, in 1886. Her most popular novel, <title>The Sorrows of Satan</title> (1895), sold more copies than any previously published English novel. After its publication, Marie Corelli became the most popular novelist in the country. \\n\\nMarie Corelli\'s work was associated with Rosicrucianism, a spiritual and cultural movement during the Victorian era. After her father\'s death, Marie Corelli continued to live with her father\'s caretaker, Bertha Vyver for over forty years. After Corelli\'s death, Bertha Vyver inherited Corelli\'s estate, served as her literary executor, and authored a biography of Corelli. Bertha Vyver was buried with Marie Corelli after her death.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"4da447ce3cd3be5bc12cc61addf152de\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1766,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_3951ac6a487a945527c5032f4a60fc56\",\"label\":\"Preferred Citation\",\"type\":\"prefercite\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"[Identification of item], Marie Corelli collection, 09916, Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"87427cee84d2e3974fdae030610cfdb6\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1767,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_4057480fa0652e38eff5f76b9d6765a6\",\"label\":\"Processing Information\",\"type\":\"processinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Lexy deGraffenreid processed this collection in Fall 2020 and Spring 2021 using the Hybrid Arrangement and Description Workflow due to the COVID-19 pandemic. She retained materials in their original folders where possible and foldered newly accessioned materials. She retained titles where previously existing and supplied folder titles where necessary. \\n\\nNeither Marie Corelli nor Bertha Vyver referred to themselves as \\\"lesbians\\\" or \\\"queer\\\" during their lifetimes. During their lifetimes, these terms were used derogatively and many terms used for queer identities today are recent and reclaimed. Out of respect for creators to self-identify, the archivist chose to not explicitly refer to Marie Corelli as a Lesbian, homosexual, or member of the LGBTQ+ community since she does not know how the creator would self-identify.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"cd3b8fb6877c4d3e0615aaaa3d8c7c63\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1768,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_29f878891c7c151e5eaeecee8984635e\",\"label\":\"Copyright Notice\",\"type\":\"userestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Photocopies of original materials may be made available for research purposes at the discretion of the Eberly Family Special Collections Library. Photocopies or reproductions of original materials may be subject to fees as outlined by the Pennsylvania State University Libraries reproduction policies.\\n\\nCopyright is retained by the creators of items in these papers, or their descendants, as stipulated by United States copyright law. Patrons seeking advice on the availability of unpublished materials for publication should consult relevant copyright law and laws of libel.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"67f13f34470758d506295fe52ea6e2ce\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1769,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_d54116aa924950bbaffdd1ce88d849d9\",\"label\":\"Custodial History\",\"type\":\"custodhist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The custodial history of this collection is unknown.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"e433bab5cbe31e8fb92273754aa5bf40\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1770,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_4cf2ec0cf4b2ab9821294c0399b2a774\",\"label\":\"Scope and Contents\",\"type\":\"scopecontent\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This collection consists of correspondence, sheet music, photographs, scrapbooks, and other ephemera produced during or after the life of the popular Victorian novelist Marie Corelli. It contains several autographed letters, most from the turn of the century. Additionally there is correspondence between publishers, memoranda of understanding, and typescripts related to publication of her works, such as <title>Master Christian</title>, <title>Tempral Power</title>, and <title>A Christmas Greeting</title>. Drafts in manuscript and typescript of J. Cumming Walters\'s texts on Corelli are also included, as are proofs of letters to the editor and deposit statements from the Library of Congress asserting copyright. This collection also includes clippings scrapbooks which document Corelli\'s many mentions in the popular press of her day. The scrapbooks were compiled topically. Scrapbook titles include as \\\"Suffrage,\\\" \\\"Free Opinions,\\\" and \\\"Death, Funerals &amp; Tributes.\\\" Also included are several letters by Charles Mackay, Corelli\'s father. As well as fliers and postcards of various productions.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"4a2daac77a82055a759e1c505ca5c869\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1771,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_a4fa07ac27e24c057494e2828fc705a8\",\"label\":\"Related Materials\",\"type\":\"relatedmaterial\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"<extref xlink:href=\\\"https://aspace.libraries.psu.edu/repositories/3/resources/1172\\\">Marie Corelli letters and postcards</extref>\\n\\n<extref xlink:href=\\\"https://archives.tricolib.brynmawr.edu/resources/bmc-m2\\\">Marie Corelli papers, Special Collections Department, Bryn Mawr College Library</extref>\\n\\n<extref xlink:href=\\\"https://archives.nypl.org/brg/19234\\\">Marie Corelli collection of papers, The Henry W. and Albert A. Berg Collection of English and American Literature, The New York Public Library</extref>\\n\\n<extref xlink:href=\\\"https://oac.cdlib.org/findaid/ark:/13030/kt2t1nb1tw/entire_text/\\\">Marie Corelli papers, UCLA Library Special Collections, Charles E. Young Research Library, University of California, Los Angeles</extref>\\n\\n<extref xlink:href=\\\"https://archives.yale.edu/repositories/11/resources/693\\\">Marie Corelli collection, Music Library, Yale University</extref>\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"8f084fec466ddef2a25faf1e03f44425\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54','2022-04-25 03:30:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1772,1,21,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"e93ae23afa9ac07dfc3d539f5447934d\",\"label\":\"Acknowledgment\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This record was shared by the Eberly Family Special Collections Library, Pennsylvania State University as a part of the ArchivesSpace Inclusive Examples project. For more information, reach out to the institution.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"18bcef688d350e36b6834abd7eb95b34\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:30:55','2022-04-25 03:30:55','2022-04-25 03:30:55',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1773,1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"3792402e179f83f77b38bae8e35f95db\",\"label\":\"General Note\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Processing Information: Processing of this series is complete.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"21362d352a921eff6bd5a09484281da2\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1774,1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"f15139a35690ff3e65c80134c2bd0b46\",\"label\":\" Restrictions on Access\",\"type\":\"accessrestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This series is open for research.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"dad3e79508905f8678de94bb586ce293\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1775,1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"ff0243b962a3b0b9181a570a60913947\",\"label\":\"Scope and content\",\"type\":\"scopecontent\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This series contains records of the Black Student Alliance as well as records of the Black History Month Planning Committee, a group that formed every year to assist with programming events for Black History Month celebrations at the University of Tennessee at Chattanooga. Most materials in this series date from 1970 to the early 1990s.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"b4e1eac040af1f6630bec7ccf963e2ea\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1776,1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"fd962678ac58ed29c3f279a25a8487f7\",\"label\":\"Terms Governing Use and Reproduction\",\"type\":\"userestrict\",\"rights_restriction\":{\"local_access_restriction_type\":[]},\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The copyright status of this series has not been evaluated..\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"3410954488448c06258cf3e1a769beb0\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1777,1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"9ff51d3dacb6d21ac5101abcd0696906\",\"label\":\"Immediate Source of Acquisition\",\"type\":\"acqinfo\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Source of acquisition--This series was deposited with the University of Tennessee at Chattanooga on an unknown date by an unknown source.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"59ae4b18a826802d2134ce438b805bc7\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1778,1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"b2d4199c8ddd2aa957d7d42ea2a3442c\",\"type\":\"bioghist\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"The Black Student Alliance is a student organization at the University of Tennessee at Chattanooga. Its mission is \\\"to define clearly and insure the rights and responsibilities of Black students, individually and collectively\\\" and \\\"to promote a continuous exchange of ideas and opinions between students and administration.\\\" The Black Student Alliance formed in 1969 under the name of the Student Human Relations Council in response to the merger of Chattanooga City College, the University of Chattanooga, and the University of Tennessee System to create the University of Tennessee at Chattanooga. This academic reorganization significantly altered the demographic of the student body, and the Student Human Relations Council was chartered as a collective group for black students. To clarify this purpose, the group changed names again in 1970 to the Black Student Association, and sponsored campus events that were both entertaining and educational. The largest of these was an annual Black Awareness Week, a full week of programming celebrating black culture. Black Awareness Week was sponsored by the Black Student Association from 1970 to 1986. In 1986, this programming shifted and expanded to align with Black History Month in February. At this point, the Dean of Students began to play a more active role in planning these events by forming a Black History Month Planning Committee each year to coordinate efforts with the Black Student Association in the celebration of Black History Month at the University of Tennessee at Chattanooga. In 2003, after a period of dormancy, the Black Student Association changed its name to the Black Student Alliance and updated its constitution. While the organizational structure changed, the group\'s mission remains the same-to serve as a liaison between the university and African-American students, and to encourage different groups to connect in idea and activity. These activities focus on maintaining African-American identity and cohesion at the university.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"b68b2e4497c89956d34b0f687d1f1f31\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1779,1,8,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"65c8576bb6e618c1f61e0c269dc19abe\",\"label\":\"Acknowledgment\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"This record was shared by the University of Tennessee at Chattanooga as a part of the ArchivesSpace Inclusive Examples project. For more information, reach out to the institution.\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"33ab81e72ffb454e9af85210b89b9444\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30','2022-04-25 03:43:30',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1780,1,NULL,4811,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_730d14bdcba16110d9d22681b9535bee\",\"label\":\"General\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Damaged\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"3e224b00df3e59cae78a1f1403d99bd5\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-28 20:22:54','2022-04-28 20:22:54','2022-04-28 20:22:54',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1781,1,NULL,5708,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_7d02a8f9d26a47cb9e3246c13c4f3f72\",\"label\":\"General\",\"type\":\"odd\",\"subnotes\":[{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_text\",\"content\":\"Damaged\",\"publish\":true,\"subnote_guid\":\"23bbd2ea502c8b1cafca8f5df5106a2f\"}]}','admin','admin','2022-04-28 20:36:22','2022-04-28 20:36:22','2022-04-28 20:36:22',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1782,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_langmaterial\",\"persistent_id\":\"6208f21e5d4c7d004d1b186f6696b691\",\"type\":\"langmaterial\",\"content\":[\"English.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-28 21:03:32','2022-04-28 21:03:32','2022-04-28 21:03:32',NULL,NULL,895,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1783,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_langmaterial\",\"persistent_id\":\"3d55fd9f309ec4db445a415db3a4480c\",\"type\":\"langmaterial\",\"content\":[\"English.\"]}','admin','admin','2022-04-28 21:05:27','2022-04-28 21:05:27','2022-04-28 21:05:27',NULL,NULL,897,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),(1784,1,NULL,5950,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,1,_binary '{\"jsonmodel_type\":\"note_multipart\",\"persistent_id\":\"aspace_366ad7f100714188c5a3e50b0